    {
      "topLeftX": 131,
      "topLeftY": 358,
      "bottomRightX": 585,
      "bottomRightY": 375,
      "content": "At the first order, this gives, both classically and quantum mechanically:",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 251,
      "topLeftY": 386,
      "bottomRightX": 603,
      "bottomRightY": 421,
      "content": "$$a _ {1} ^ {2} (n) \\quad \\text { or } \\quad a ^ {2} (n, n - 1) = \\frac {n + \\operatorname{const} h}{\\pi m \\omega_ {0}} \\tag {46}$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 130,
      "topLeftY": 431,
      "bottomRightX": 605,
      "bottomRightY": 497,
      "content": "Quantum theoretically, the constant in Eq. 46 can be determined with the condition that $a(n_0, n_0 - 1)$ must vanish in the ground state. If we number n such a way that n is equal to zero in the ground state i.e. $n_0 = 0$, then it follows that",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 303,
      "topLeftY": 497,
      "bottomRightX": 603,
      "bottomRightY": 531,
      "content": "$$a ^ {2} (n, n - 1) = \\frac {n h}{\\pi m \\omega_ {0}} \\tag {47}$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 130,
      "topLeftY": 535,
      "bottomRightX": 605,
      "bottomRightY": 587,
      "content": "Thus it follows from the recursion equations 42 that in the classical theory $a_{\\tau}$ (to first order in $\\lambda$) has the form $\\chi(\\tau)n^{\\frac{\\tau}{2}}$ where $\\chi(\\tau)$ represents a factor independent from $n$. In the quantum theory, Eq. 43 implies",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 270,
      "topLeftY": 600,
      "bottomRightX": 603,
      "bottomRightY": 639,
      "content": "$$a (n, n - \\tau) = \\chi (\\tau) \\sqrt {\\frac {n !}{(n - \\tau) !}} \\quad , \\tag {48}$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 130,
      "topLeftY": 650,
      "bottomRightX": 605,
      "bottomRightY": 701,
      "content": "where $\\chi(\\tau)$ represents itself a proportionality factor independent from $n$. Naturally, for large values of $n$, the quantum theoretical value of $a_{\\tau}$ tends asymptotically to the classical one.",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 151,
      "topLeftY": 703,
      "bottomRightX": 496,
      "bottomRightY": 719,
      "content": "For the energy, it is obvious to try the classical Ansatz",
